> The warning you're encountering is related to the use of Variable Length Arrays (VLAs) in C++. VLAs are not a standard feature in C++, and using them can lead to portability issues, since they are a compiler extension (supported by Clang but not by all compilers).
> 
> Here are a couple of alternatives you could consider:
> 
> 1. **Use `std::vector`:**
> 
>    You can use `std::vector` for dynamic memory allocation without manually handling memory deallocation. The `std::vector` automatically manages its own memory.
> 
>    ```cpp
>    #include <vector>
>    #include <string>
> 
>    template<typename T>
>    T reverseAndCast(const std::string& elt) {
>        std::vector<char> swap(elt.length());
>        for (unsigned ii = 0; ii < elt.length(); ii++) {
>            swap[elt.length() - ii - 1] = elt[ii];
>        }
>        return *reinterpret_cast<T*>(swap.data());
>    }
>    ```
> 
>    In this example, `swap.data()` gives you a pointer to the underlying array managed by the vector.
> 
> 2. **Use a fixed-size array or stack memory, if possible:**
> 
>    If you know the maximum size that `elt` will ever be, and it's reasonable, you could use a fixed-size array. Otherwise, using `std::vector` or another dynamic allocation approach is safer.
> 
> 3. **Avoid Type-Punning Across Incompatible Types:**
> 
>    It's important to ensure that the type casting you are performing (`reinterpret_cast`) is safe and well-defined. Type-punning against incompatible types can lead to undefined behavior. If `T` is larger than `char`, ensure that the memory alignment and size assumptions are valid.
> 
>    If `T` can vary in size or alignment significantly, consider a more robust design or abstraction to handle such cases.
